    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go into a bit more detail about the actual people involved in this shift? Like, what are the typical profiles of the people moving into these revitalized areas versus the people who usually end up leaving?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, people often referred to as \"newcomers\" in revitalized neighborhoods tend to be younger, often from more affluent backgrounds, and may include a higher percentage of educated professionals looking for modern amenities and a vibrant community. These individuals are sometimes drawn to nearby urban centers, trendy restaurants, boutique shops, and multicultural neighborhoods with a strong arts and social scene.\n\nOn the flip side, the original residents—often referred to as \"presenters\"—usually have deep roots in the community, a diverse range of socioeconomic backgrounds, and may be facing an array of challenges that make sustaining in a gentrifying neighborhood difficult. These presenters typically have lower income levels, lower educational attainment, and may be working blue-collar jobs. The rising cost of living and changing neighborhood dynamics, like a loss of local businesses that cater to them, can make gentrification a challenging process for these long-term residents. Such residents might opt to move elsewhere if they're unable to afford the increasing costs and feel disconnected from the new culture or climate of the neighborhood.",
      "source": "target",
